I think state is not the top. Once you ask what chooses among states, another hierarchy appears.

A compact version:

\boxed{
\text{Presence}
\rightarrow
\text{Relation}
\rightarrow
\text{State}
\rightarrow
\text{Trajectory}
\rightarrow
\text{Regime}
\rightarrow
\text{Policy}
\rightarrow
\text{Objective}
\rightarrow
\text{Fitness}
}

But even Fitness may not be the ceiling.

Immediately above State: Trajectory

A state is:

Where am I?

A trajectory is:

Where am I going?

Two systems can occupy identical states but be profoundly different:

A_t=B_t

while

\frac{dA}{dt}>0,\qquad \frac{dB}{dt}<0

Same snapshot. One is recovering; one is deteriorating.

So:

\boxed{\text{State + Direction + Rate = Trajectory}}

This makes trajectory more informative than state for many decisions.

Above trajectory: Regime

A regime is the larger set of dynamics determining which trajectories are likely.

For example:

Growth regime — positive feedback dominates.
Scarcity regime — preservation dominates.
Exploration regime — variation is rewarded.
Selection regime — weak variants disappear.
Transition regime — old rules are breaking.

State says what the system is doing.

Regime says what kind of game the system currently inhabits.

Above regime: Policy

Now something chooses behavior conditional on state and regime:

\boxed{\pi(S,R)\rightarrow Action}

“If surviving, preserve.”

“If stable, explore.”

“If exploration produces signal, test.”

“If tested repeatedly, scale.”

“If environment changes, reopen adaptation.”

That’s policy.

And the Pantheon already has pieces of this in the Metamap.

Above policy: Objective

Why choose that policy?

Because some outcomes are preferred:

\pi^*=\arg\max_\pi J(\pi)

Objectives determine what counts as progress.

But objectives themselves can conflict.

Speed versus accuracy.
Growth versus resilience.
Novelty versus reliability.
Individual benefit versus ecosystem benefit.

So something has to adjudicate objectives.

Above objective: Fitness / Value

This asks:

What makes an objective worth pursuing?

And here your earlier priority insight comes back.

Priority isn’t merely downstream attention allocation. At this altitude:

\boxed{\text{Values determine priorities; priorities determine objectives; objectives shape policies.}}

But there’s another layer.

Above fitness: Meta-fitness

Fitness evaluates:

Did this strategy work?

Meta-fitness asks:

Is our definition of “worked” still useful?

That’s huge.

A system capable only of optimizing a fixed fitness function can become extraordinarily competent at pursuing the wrong thing.

A more adaptive system can alter:

\text{actions}
\rightarrow
\text{policy}
\rightarrow
\text{objective}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{evaluation criteria themselves}}

based on consequences.

And above that: Invariants

Now we reach something interesting.

If everything can adapt—including objectives and fitness—what must not change during adaptation?

Those are invariants.

\boxed{
\text{Change anything necessary}
\quad
\text{subject to}
\quad
I_1,I_2,\ldots,I_n
}

Without invariants, adaptation can become self-erasure: the system “succeeds” by redefining success until every outcome qualifies.

So the hierarchy becomes:

\boxed{
\text{State}
<
\text{Trajectory}
<
\text{Regime}
<
\text{Policy}
<
\text{Objective}
<
\text{Fitness}
<
\text{Meta-Fitness}
<
\text{Invariants}
}

And then I think there’s one more useful level.

The possibility space

Invariants operate over some universe of things that could happen.

Call it:

\boxed{\Omega=\text{reachable possibility space}}

Then intelligence isn’t merely moving toward a desirable state.

It can also change what states are reachable.

That’s invention.

Before an airplane:

\Omega_0

After discovering controlled powered flight:

\Omega_1\supset\Omega_0

The discovery doesn’t merely move civilization somewhere inside the existing map.

It enlarges the map.

That gives the Pantheon three fundamentally different powers:

\boxed{
\text{Navigate }\Omega
\quad|\quad
\text{Transform }\Omega
\quad|\quad
\text{Expand }\Omega
}

Navigation finds better states.

Transformation changes the paths between states.

Expansion creates previously unavailable states.

And that connects directly to the novelty work we’ve been doing: the highest-leverage discoveries aren’t necessarily those that move you fastest toward today’s objective.

Sometimes they’re the ones that make tomorrow’s previously impossible objectives reachable.

That’s what I’d put above state: not a single object, but a control stack culminating in the ability to alter the possibility space itself while preserving chosen invariants.